{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Young1963-gold-6layer",
  "Source": "L. Young, \"Multilayer reflection coatings on a metal mirror,\" Appl. Opt. 2, 445-447 (1963). doi:10.1364/AO.2.000445 - 'Solution of the Problem', p. 447 (band-centre wavelength) with the Fig. 1 stack",
  "Notes": "6-layer quarter-wave ZnS/cryolite enhancement of a gold laser-cavity mirror for high reflectance at both the ruby line (0.7 um) and the Nd line (1.06 um).\nSpecification: Highest possible (and equal) reflectance at 0.7 um and 1.06 um.\nReported: Text p. 447: f = 0.785 and 1.19 give R = 0.9925 at both lines (no gain over four layers, more critical to fabricate). The band-centre wavelength is not printed; reference_nm = 0.7 x 1.19 = 0.833 um (1.06 x 0.785 = 0.8321 um) is derived here.\nNotes: Fig. 1 and the Fig. 2 inset show the stack from the incident medium (n = 1) as ZnS (high, n1) / cryolite (low, n2) / ... / cryolite / metal, i.e. the low-index layer is next to the metal and the high-index layer faces the light; listed here in that order. All layers are quarter waves at reference_nm with non-absorbing constant indices ZnS 2.2, cryolite 1.32. Normal incidence, vacuum/air incident medium. The gold substrate is described in the paper only by its reflectance (97 % at 0.7 um, 98.2 % at 1.06 um, AIP Handbook), n ~ 0.3 near 1 um, and the n = 132 lossless equivalent medium; 'Au' library data is used here, so recomputed R will differ somewhat (gold k is not zero, making the response asymmetric, as the paper notes). The paper uses 0.7 um for the ruby line (actual 694.3 nm). Six-layer case considered but not chosen by the author.\nLayers from 'Solution of the Problem', p. 447 (band-centre wavelength) with the Fig. 1 stack; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), compare with the paper: ours R 98.4 % at 700 nm, 99.4 % at 1060 nm (Johnson & Christy gold); paper predicted 99.25 % at both. Johnson & Christy gold here; the paper characterises its gold only by its reflectance (97 % at 0.7 um, 98.2 % at 1.06 um).",
